## Core Product Flow

A user selects 5 movies they like and provides ratings/preferences for them.

The system uses those selected movies to:

1. Fetch similar candidate movies using vector search.
2. Combine user preference information and movie information.
3. Pass this data into a PyTorch neural network.
4. Recommend the top K movies to the user.

The recommendation flow should be practical and system-design oriented, not only model-focused.

## Dataset

Use the MovieLens dataset.

Important dataset files may include:

- `ratings.csv`
- `movies.csv`
- `tags.csv`
- `links.csv`
- `genome-scores.csv`
- `genome-tags.csv`

The ratings dataset should be treated as event data and streamed into the system using Kafka.

## Streaming Design

MovieLens ratings should be converted into simulated streaming events.

Kafka will be used to stream rating events into the system.

Each rating event may contain:

- user ID
- movie ID
- rating
- timestamp

This makes the project more realistic because real recommender systems often receive user interactions continuously.

The model may later support periodic retraining based on newly collected ratings.

## Storage

Use OpenSearch as the vector database.

Movie information should be embedded and stored in OpenSearch.

Movie information may include:

- title
- genres
- tags
- genome tag relevance
- external metadata if added later

OpenSearch should support vector search for retrieving similar movies.

If another database is needed, prefer PostgreSQL.

PostgreSQL can be used for structured data such as:

- users
- ratings
- movie metadata
- recommendation logs
- model input/output records

## Candidate Generation

When a user provides movies they like, the system should first fetch similar movies from OpenSearch.

This retrieved set acts as candidate movies.

Later, we may add randomness/exploration so recommendations are not always too deterministic.

Do not over-engineer randomness yet. Leave it as a future design decision.

## Ranking Model

Use a neural network built with PyTorch.

The PyTorch model should rank or score candidate movies for the user.

The model input may include:

- user preference features
- selected liked movies
- movie embeddings
- movie genres
- rating-derived features
- candidate movie metadata

The exact architecture can evolve over time.

Start simple and improve gradually.

## Data Processing

Use pandas for data cleaning, exploration, feature engineering, and batch processing.

Pandas can be used to:

- load MovieLens CSV files
- inspect missing values
- merge ratings with movie metadata
- create training datasets
- prepare candidate/ranking features
- simulate streaming input from historical timestamps

## Deployment and Engineering

Use Docker for containerizing services.

Potential services may include:

- Kafka
- OpenSearch
- backend API
- model service
- PostgreSQL
- frontend, if added later

CI/CD may be added later.

Kubernetes may be added later.

Do not assume Kubernetes or CI/CD are required immediately, but design the project so they can be added cleanly.

## Possible Backend

The backend can expose APIs such as:

- submit selected movies and ratings
- fetch recommendations
- ingest rating events
- retrieve similar movies
- trigger model inference
- inspect health/status

FastAPI is a good default choice if a backend framework is needed.

## Monitoring and Observability

Monitoring and observability will be added later.

Potential tools may include:

- Prometheus
- Grafana
- OpenTelemetry
- logging
- model/data drift checks
- recommendation quality metrics

Do not finalize the observability stack yet.

Leave room for future design.

## Evaluation Ideas

Offline recommender evaluation may include:

- Precision@K
- Recall@K
- NDCG@K
- MAP@K

Online/system metrics may include:

- latency
- throughput
- error rate
- recommendation request volume
- cost per prediction
- model inference time

These do not all need to be implemented immediately.

## Project Philosophy

Build this project incrementally.

Prefer a clean, working MVP over an over-engineered system.

The project should be useful for a resume and ML engineering interviews.

When suggesting code or architecture, prioritize:

- clarity
- modularity
- realistic ML system design
- practical tradeoffs
- beginner-friendly explanations
- production-inspired structure without unnecessary complexity

## Important Design Reminder

The main user-facing flow is:

User selects 5 liked movies
-> system retrieves similar movies from OpenSearch
-> candidate movies are passed with user/movie features into a PyTorch model
-> system returns top K recommendations.

Keep this flow in mind when generating code, suggesting architecture, or creating files.
